{% comment %}
  A widget's mount point, its data, and the lazy loader for its script.

  Shared by every block that renders through Preact, so the loading rules live
  in one place. `{% render %}` runs in an isolated scope — `block` is NOT
  visible here — so everything arrives as a parameter.

  ── What goes on the page ──

    <div id="sje-{layout}-{id}" data-sje-widget-id="{id}">   the mount point
    window.SJE.widgets[id]                                   the widget
    window.SJE.widgets[id].media[]                           every media it plays

  ONE object per widget, holding everything — every mp4 rung, the preview clip
  and its rungs, the thumbnail, the tagged products. There is no separate
  `SJE.media` lookup any more: a widget that has to be joined against a global
  map before it can be drawn is exactly the retrieval the stored copy exists to
  remove, and keeping both put the same bytes on the page twice.

  `media` is IN PLAY ORDER, so it is the order too — there is no `mediaIds` on
  this object to disagree with it.

  The component never fetches: by the time its script runs, everything it needs
  is already inlined above it. A widget saved before the copy existed has an
  empty `media` in storage, and only then are its medias looked up one metafield
  at a time and pushed on. The copy is kept current by the app: editing a video
  rewrites it in every widget that plays it, and deleting one takes it off them.

  ── Loading ──

  `window.SJE.scripts` is keyed BY LAYOUT, so a page holding a carousel and a
  story bar fetches two scripts, while a page holding three carousels fetches
  one. That is the "load it once" rule: once per layout, not once per block.

  The fetch waits for a sign of a real visitor — a mouse crossing the page, a
  pointer, a key, a scroll —
  or for a mount point to appear after load (a theme-editor re-render, or a
  section loaded on demand). Whichever comes first wins.

  Accepts:
    layout    - carousel | story-bar | …  (matches the id and `mountAll`)
    script    - the asset filename, e.g. sje-carousel.js
    widget_id - the widget's 6-digit id
    widget    - the widget's metafield value, already resolved by the block
    settings  - the block's own `block.settings`, optional

  ── Why settings ride on the ELEMENT ──

  The widget goes on `window.SJE`, keyed by id, because it is the merchant's
  content and every block showing it shows the same thing. Settings are the
  opposite: they are one placement's appearance, and two carousels of the same
  widget can be set up differently. So they are written on the mount point
  itself, and `readSettings` in the script reads them per mount.

  Serialised whole rather than one attribute per setting, so a new setting
  needs a line in the block's `{% schema %}` and nothing here.
{% endcomment %}

{%- comment -%}
  `display:block` is deliberate: this theme hides empty elements
  (`div:empty { display: none }`), and a mount point is empty until Preact
  fills it — without this the script would have nothing to render into.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div
  id="sje-{{ layout }}-{{ widget_id }}"
  class="sje-mount sje-mount--{{ layout }}"
  data-sje-widget-id="{{ widget_id }}"
  {%- if settings != blank -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      `escape` because `json` emits double quotes and this sits inside a
      double-quoted attribute. The browser decodes them back before the script
      ever sees the value.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    data-sje-settings="{{ settings | json | escape }}"
  {%- endif -%}
  style="display:block;height:100%;"
>
  {%- comment -%}
    ── The server-side skeleton ──

    The script is lazy: it is not fetched until the shopper's first pointer,
    key or scroll (see below), so without this the block draws its heading and
    then 70vh of nothing until they touch the page. These placeholders fill
    that gap and are the merchant's promise that something is coming.

    Safe to leave inside the mount point. Preact's `render()` collects the
    container's existing children as `excessDomChildren` and removes whatever
    it did not itself claim, so the first render replaces this outright — no
    hydration, no double-draw, and nothing here to clean up by hand.

    Styled INLINE rather than left to `.sje-widget--carousel .sje-widget__item`
    in the stylesheet, for the same reason everything else in this extension
    is: the sheet has failed to load on the storefront before, and a skeleton
    that depends on it for its HEIGHT disappears when it does — which is the
    exact failure this is here to prevent.

    ⚠️ The `&nbsp;` is LOAD-BEARING. Themes commonly hide empty elements
    (`div:empty { display: none }`) and a placeholder has no content of its
    own; `font-size:0;line-height:0` keeps the character from taking up space.
    Same rule as `sje-widget.liquid`, CLAUDE.md §6.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign sje_sp = 'var(--sje-standard-spacing, 8px)' -%}
  {%- capture sje_bone -%}
    background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);border-radius:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 1);font-size:0;line-height:0;
  {%- endcapture -%}

  {%- if layout == 'story-bar' -%}
    {%- capture sje_row -%}display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 2);overflow:hidden;{%- endcapture -%}
    {%- capture sje_bone_shape -%}flex:0 0 calc({{ sje_sp }} * 9);height:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 9);border-radius:50%;{%- endcapture -%}
  {%- else -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      ⚠️ The row must be exactly as tall as the REAL one, or the cards resize
      the moment the script takes over — which is the jump a skeleton exists to
      prevent, made worse by being the one thing the shopper is looking at.

      `Carousel` is a column: the scrolling row takes `flex:1 1 auto`, and the
      arrows under it take `flex:0 0 auto` at `paddingBlockStart: sp(1.5)` plus
      a button `arrow_scale`% of one spacing tall. So the row is the group less
      that strip — and the strip is nothing at all when the merchant has set
      the arrows to Hidden.

      These three numbers are the pairing: `GAP_STEPS`, the `paddingBlockStart`
      and `arrowSize` in `Carousel.tsx`. Change one there, change it here.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign sje_arrows = settings.arrow_position | default: 'center' -%}
    {%- if sje_arrows == 'hidden' -%}
      {%- capture sje_row_height -%}100%{%- endcapture -%}
    {%- else -%}
      {%- assign sje_arrow_mult = settings.arrow_scale | default: 500 | divided_by: 100.0 -%}
      {%- capture sje_row_height -%}calc(100% - {{ sje_sp }} * 1.5 - {{ sje_sp }} * {{ sje_arrow_mult }}){%- endcapture -%}
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- capture sje_row -%}display:flex;align-items:stretch;gap:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 1.5);height:{{ sje_row_height }};overflow:hidden;{%- endcapture -%}
    {%- capture sje_bone_shape -%}flex:0 0 auto;height:100%;width:auto;aspect-ratio:9 / 16;{%- endcapture -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  <div class="sje-widget__items" style="{{ sje_row }}" aria-hidden="true">
    {%- comment -%}
      One per video the widget actually plays, so the row is the right length
      the moment the script takes over and nothing shifts. `limit` because a
      widget of forty is still only a screenful of placeholders.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- for sje_bone_item in widget.media limit: 8 -%}
      <div class="sje-widget__item" style="{{ sje_bone }}{{ sje_bone_shape }}">&nbsp;</div>
    {%- endfor -%}
  </div>
</div>

<script>
  (function () {
    var SJE = (window.SJE = window.SJE || {});
    SJE.widgets = SJE.widgets || {};
    SJE.scripts = SJE.scripts || {};

    {%- comment -%}
      Written field by field rather than as `{{ widget | json }}` so that
      `media` can come from the copy when there is one and be filled in from
      the old per-media metafields when there is not — and so that nothing
      the storefront has no use for rides along.

      No `mediaIds`: `media` is already in play order, and a second list
      saying the same thing is one more thing that can disagree with it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    SJE.widgets[{{ widget_id | json }}] = {
      id: {{ widget_id | json }},
      name: {{ widget.name | json }},
      layout: {{ widget.layout | json }},
      enabled: {{ widget.enabled | json }},
      shuffle: {{ widget.shuffle | json }},
      updatedAt: {{ widget.updatedAt | json }},
      media: {% if widget.media.size > 0 %}{{ widget.media | json }}{% else %}[]{% endif %}
    };

    {%- if widget.media.size == 0 -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The old path, for widgets saved before the copy existed. Pushed one at
        a time rather than built as a literal: an id whose metafield has gone
        is skipped, and skipping entries inside an array literal means getting
        the commas right around the gaps. These widgets move over to the copy
        on their next save in the app.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- for sje_media_id in widget.mediaIds -%}
        {%- assign sje_media_key = 'media_' | append: sje_media_id -%}
        {%- assign sje_media = app.metafields.shoppable_videos[sje_media_key].value -%}
        {%- if sje_media != blank -%}
          SJE.widgets[{{ widget_id | json }}].media.push({{ sje_media | json }});
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    var LAYOUT = {{ layout | json }};
    var SRC = {{ script | asset_url | json }};

    if (SJE.scripts[LAYOUT]) return;
    SJE.scripts[LAYOUT] = "idle";

    {%- comment -%}
      `mouseover` first, because it is the earliest of these a mouse user can
      produce: it fires the moment the pointer crosses into the page, before
      they have decided to click, scroll or reach the widget. The script is
      then already there when they arrive at it.

      Deliberately NOT `mousemove` — that fires continuously and would only
      arrive at the same conclusion a fraction sooner, and not
      `pointerover`, which a tap also raises and would make `touchstart`
      redundant while being harder to reason about on a phone.

      Every one of these is `once` and every one removes the rest on the first
      to fire, so the list is a race, not five listeners left running.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    var EVENTS = ["mouseover", "pointerdown", "keydown", "touchstart", "wheel", "scroll"];
    var observer = null;

    function load() {
      if (SJE.scripts[LAYOUT] !== "idle") return;
      SJE.scripts[LAYOUT] = "loading";

      EVENTS.forEach(function (name) {
        window.removeEventListener(name, load);
      });
      if (observer) observer.disconnect();

      var el = document.createElement("script");
      el.src = SRC;
      el.defer = true;
      el.onerror = function () { SJE.scripts[LAYOUT] = "error"; };
      document.head.appendChild(el);
    }

    SJE.load = SJE.load || function (layout) { if (layout === LAYOUT) load(); };

    EVENTS.forEach(function (name) {
      window.addEventListener(name, load, { once: true, passive: true });
    });

    if (window.MutationObserver) {
      observer = new MutationObserver(function (records) {
        for (var i = 0; i < records.length; i++) {
          var added = records[i].addedNodes;
          for (var j = 0; j < added.length; j++) {
            var node = added[j];
            if (node.nodeType !== 1) continue;
            if (
              (node.matches && node.matches("[data-sje-widget-id]")) ||
              (node.querySelector && node.querySelector("[data-sje-widget-id]"))
            ) {
              load();
              return;
            }
          }
        }
      });
      observer.observe(document.documentElement, { childList: true, subtree: true });
    }
  })();
</script>
